WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
In 1985, British officials doubted that a boy

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:06.560
returning from Ghana was really his mother's son.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.080
A geneticist, Alec Jeffreys, had just found a

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.880
way to compare people's DNA as patterns of

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:13.600
bands.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.720
He compared the boy's pattern with his family's,

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.440
and it showed he really was her son.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.360
So how can a pattern of

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.520
bands show who's related?

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.920
And how is that different from reading DNA

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.200
one base at a time?

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.160
There are two main ways to get that

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.320
kind of evidence.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.200
DNA sequencing reads the exact order of bases,

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.040
the A's, T's, C's and G's, along a

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:39.440
stretch of DNA.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:43.120
DNA profiling doesn't read the order at all.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:45.840
It compares a few chosen places where people's

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:47.760
DNA tends to differ.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.400
So one reads every letter, and the other

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.560
checks a handful of markers.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:54.560
Which one you use depends on the question

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:55.360
you're asking.

00:00:56.567 --> 00:00:59.446
Sequencing never reads a whole chromosome in one

00:00:59.527 --> 00:01:00.166
go.

00:01:00.487 --> 00:01:03.446
First, the DNA is copied and broken into

00:01:03.527 --> 00:01:04.846
short fragments.

00:01:05.127 --> 00:01:07.766
A machine reads each fragment one base at

00:01:07.807 --> 00:01:10.246
a time, because each kind of base is

00:01:10.407 --> 00:01:12.486
tagged with its own coloured dye.

00:01:12.807 --> 00:01:15.366
The copies were broken at different places, so

00:01:15.407 --> 00:01:17.006
the fragments overlap.

00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:20.166
Software lines up those overlaps, like matching the

00:01:20.247 --> 00:01:23.126
edges of torn strips, and rebuilds the whole

00:01:23.287 --> 00:01:24.006
sequence.

00:01:25.234 --> 00:01:28.913
Because sequencing reads every base, it finds single-base

00:01:28.954 --> 00:01:31.873
differences directly, like the snips in the frequency

00:01:31.954 --> 00:01:32.713
video.

00:01:33.074 --> 00:01:35.553
Take the sickle cell allele, where one A

00:01:35.634 --> 00:01:37.073
is changed to T.

00:01:37.394 --> 00:01:39.713
Sequence this family at that spot.

00:01:39.794 --> 00:01:42.833
Both parents are unaffected, but each shows two

00:01:42.914 --> 00:01:45.793
letters there, A and T, so each is

00:01:45.834 --> 00:01:46.993
a carrier.

00:01:47.034 --> 00:01:49.793
Their affected daughter shows only T.

00:01:49.874 --> 00:01:52.353
That's the recessive pattern, now seen in the

00:01:52.434 --> 00:01:54.113
DNA itself.

00:01:54.354 --> 00:01:56.513
Sequence thousands of people this way, and you

00:01:56.594 --> 00:01:59.633
get the allele's frequency across a whole population.

00:02:00.834 --> 00:02:04.113
Today, profiling usually looks at markers called short

00:02:04.274 --> 00:02:05.393
tandem repeats.

00:02:05.954 --> 00:02:08.113
Each one is a short run of bases,

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:11.873
like G, A, T, A, repeated over and

00:02:12.034 --> 00:02:13.953
over, side by side.

00:02:14.034 --> 00:02:16.193
The number of repeats varies from person to

00:02:16.434 --> 00:02:17.233
person.

00:02:17.354 --> 00:02:19.073
One copy of a marker might have eight

00:02:19.154 --> 00:02:21.873
repeats, and the other copy eleven.

00:02:21.954 --> 00:02:24.753
You carry two copies of every marker, one

00:02:24.834 --> 00:02:27.393
from each parent, so your repeat numbers are

00:02:27.474 --> 00:02:29.553
inherited, just like alleles.

00:02:30.767 --> 00:02:33.966
To compare lengths, labs copy each marker many

00:02:34.047 --> 00:02:36.886
times, then run the copies through a gel.

00:02:37.247 --> 00:02:40.846
DNA is negatively charged, so an electric current

00:02:41.007 --> 00:02:42.766
pulls it towards the positive end.

00:02:43.327 --> 00:02:45.406
The gel is a jelly full of tiny

00:02:45.567 --> 00:02:46.126
pores.

00:02:46.687 --> 00:02:48.846
Every piece carries the same charge for its

00:02:48.927 --> 00:02:52.286
length, so the pores decide the speed: shorter

00:02:52.487 --> 00:02:54.766
pieces slip through more easily, so they travel

00:02:54.847 --> 00:02:55.646
further.

00:02:56.047 --> 00:02:58.366
Each length ends up as a band, and

00:02:58.447 --> 00:03:01.086
a person's set of bands is their profile.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:04.460
Here's how profiles show inheritance.

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:07.660
At every marker, a child has one copy

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:09.820
from the mother and one from the father.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:13.340
So every band in the child's profile must

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:15.420
match a band in one parent or the

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:16.140
other.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:19.180
Here, the child's eight came from the mother,

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:21.020
and the twelve came from the father.

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:23.340
So if a child has a band that

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:26.140
neither the mother nor a man has, that

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:28.300
man can be ruled out as the father.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.300
A match is weaker evidence than a mismatch,

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.700
because lots of people share any one band

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:35.740
pattern.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:37.980
Say each marker's pattern is shared by one

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:39.500
person in ten.

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:42.380
Matching one marker means very little.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:44.940
But if the markers are inherited separately, the

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:46.780
chances multiply.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:49.420
Three markers give one in a thousand, and

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.460
ten give one in ten billion.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:55.740
That's why real profiles compare many markers, and

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:58.700
even then, a match is strong evidence, not

00:03:58.780 --> 00:03:59.420
proof.

00:04:00.634 --> 00:04:03.113
So which tool fits which question?

00:04:03.594 --> 00:04:05.713
If you need a particular base, like a

00:04:05.754 --> 00:04:08.313
disease allele or a snip, sequence it.

00:04:08.794 --> 00:04:11.433
Profiling can't see it, because it only measures

00:04:11.674 --> 00:04:14.553
repeat lengths, mostly in DNA that doesn't code

00:04:14.634 --> 00:04:15.433
for a protein.

00:04:15.994 --> 00:04:17.513
If you need to know who's related to

00:04:17.674 --> 00:04:20.873
whom, profiling is quick and cheap across many

00:04:21.194 --> 00:04:21.913
individuals.

00:04:22.154 --> 00:04:25.273
That's how conservation programs check relatedness in captive

00:04:25.474 --> 00:04:27.113
animals before choosing mates.

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:30.173
But each tool has limits.

00:04:30.574 --> 00:04:33.293
A profile is a handful of markers, not

00:04:33.374 --> 00:04:35.613
a whole genome, so it can't tell you

00:04:35.734 --> 00:04:36.853
about genes.

00:04:37.134 --> 00:04:39.933
And finding a variant by sequencing still doesn't

00:04:40.054 --> 00:04:42.333
tell you what it does, or prove that

00:04:42.414 --> 00:04:43.853
it causes a disease.

00:04:45.067 --> 00:04:46.426
Three things to remember.

00:04:46.987 --> 00:04:51.546
Sequence means bases, and profile means selected markers.

00:04:51.867 --> 00:04:54.906
DNA runs to red, the positive lead.

00:04:55.027 --> 00:04:57.306
And every band in a child must come

00:04:57.387 --> 00:04:58.666
from Mum or Dad.

00:04:59.867 --> 00:05:01.546
Two harder questions now.

00:05:02.107 --> 00:05:05.386
First, this gel shows a mother, her child

00:05:05.467 --> 00:05:07.706
and two men, at three markers.

00:05:08.347 --> 00:05:10.826
Which man can be excluded as the child's

00:05:11.027 --> 00:05:11.306
father?

00:05:11.867 --> 00:05:13.386
Use the bands to explain.

00:05:13.947 --> 00:05:15.306
Pause here to answer.

00:05:25.734 --> 00:05:28.533
First, find the child's bands that didn't come

00:05:28.614 --> 00:05:29.813
from the mother.

00:05:29.854 --> 00:05:32.373
They're the twelve at marker one, the thirteen

00:05:32.414 --> 00:05:34.773
at marker two, and the ten at marker

00:05:34.934 --> 00:05:35.973
three.

00:05:36.134 --> 00:05:38.453
Man A has all three, so he can't

00:05:38.534 --> 00:05:39.813
be excluded.

00:05:39.894 --> 00:05:42.373
Man B lacks the twelve and the ten,

00:05:42.454 --> 00:05:44.133
so he's excluded.

00:05:44.214 --> 00:05:46.293
If you said man A is definitely the

00:05:46.494 --> 00:05:49.173
father, a match only means he can't be

00:05:49.254 --> 00:05:49.973
ruled out.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:51.680
Second.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:55.600
Researchers want to know whether a disease-linked variant,

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:57.920
a change in a single base, is more

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:00.800
common in one population than another.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:04.160
Explain why DNA sequencing is more useful than

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:06.880
DNA profiling for this question.

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:09.920
Then name one question where profiling would be

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.360
the better choice.

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:12.720
Pause here to answer.

00:06:23.134 --> 00:06:25.533
The variant is one base, so you need

00:06:25.574 --> 00:06:27.333
the base order at that spot.

00:06:27.614 --> 00:06:30.493
Sequencing reads it in everyone tested, so you

00:06:30.574 --> 00:06:33.733
can compare the allele's frequency in each population.

00:06:34.014 --> 00:06:37.133
Profiling only measures repeat lengths, so it can't

00:06:37.214 --> 00:06:38.093
show that base.

00:06:38.654 --> 00:06:41.453
Profiling is better for relatedness, like a paternity

00:06:41.534 --> 00:06:41.933
test.

00:06:42.494 --> 00:06:44.413
And if you said profiling reads the whole

00:06:44.494 --> 00:06:46.173
genome, it doesn't.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:49.320
So, start with the question.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:51.000
Need a particular base?

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:52.120
Sequence it.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:53.640
Need to know who's related?

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:57.480
Compare profiles, and remember that one mismatch can

00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:00.520
exclude, but a match needs many markers.

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:03.320
Next, we'll see what huge collaborative

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:06.720
projects, with DNA data from thousands of people,

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:08.760
can show about whole populations.

00:07:09.967 --> 00:07:11.406
Here are the key notes.

00:07:11.647 --> 00:07:13.566
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:13.647 --> 00:07:14.446
to copy them.
